ps://www.dirk.nl/winkels/rotterdam/hellevoetstraat/86","ph":"+31 88 313 4665"},{"n":"Distributionpoint Flinck on-demand delivery service","g":"essentials","k":"post_office","la":51.916182,"lo":4.499258,"d":1322},{"n":"Sizzling Crackers","g":"essentials","k":"hairdresser","la":51.917847,"lo":4.478921,"d":1327,"wifi":1},{"n":"ALDI","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":51.916333,"lo":4.499788,"d":1358},{"n":"Rabobank","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":51.918323,"lo":4.479284,"d":1365,"h":"Mo 13:00-17:00; Tu-Fr 10:00-17:00","w":"https://www.rabobank.nl/lokale-bank/rotterdam"},{"n":"Bibliotheek","g":"essentials","k":"library","la":51.913541,"lo":4.469195,"d":1421,"h":"Mo-Fr 09:00-17:00","w":"https://hr.nl/bibliotheek","wifi":1},{"n":"Albert Heijn","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":51.918976,"lo":4.47687,"d":1502,"h":"Mo-Th 08:00-20:00; Fr 08:00-21:00; Sa 08:00-20:00; Su 12:00-18:00","w":"https://www.ah.nl/winkel/1540","ph":"+31 10 413 7133"},{"n":"KPN Tower","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.907,"lo":4.489,"d":137,"ds":"KPN Tower is a 96.5-metre-tall (317 ft) office building in Rotterdam (a city in the Netherlands). It was designed by Italian architect Renzo Piano and construction began in 1998. The building was officially opened by its owner KPN on September 28, 2000.","wpu":"KPN Tower","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Toren_op_Zuid.JPG/960px-Toren_op_Zuid.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Erasmusbrug","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.909167,"lo":4.486667,"d":242,"ds":"The Erasmusbrug (English: \"Erasmus Bridge\") is a combined cable-stayed and bascule bridge. Construction began in 1986 and was completed in 1996. It crosses the Nieuwe Maas in the centre of Rotterdam, connecting the north and south parts of the city.","wpu":"Erasmusbrug","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Erasmusbrug%2C_September_2019.jpg/960px-Erasmusbrug%2C_September_2019.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Maastoren","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.908731,"lo":4.493417,"d":480,"ds":"The Maastoren (Dutch for Meuse Tower) is with its height of 165 metres (541 feet) the second tallest building in the Netherlands. The office building is situated on the bank of the Nieuwe Maas, after which the building is named, in the neighborhood Kop van Zuid in the South Holland city of Rotterdam. The Maastoren was built between 2006 and 2009, and had OVG as its developer.","wpu":"Maastoren","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Maastoren_2012.jpg/960px-Maastoren_2012.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"FENIX Museum of Migration","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.9022,"lo":4.4843,"d":564,"ds":"The FENIX Museum of Migration is a museum dedicated to human migration, located within the former Fenix warehouse in Rotterdam, the Netherlands. It was opened on 16 May 2025. The museum has two inaugural exhibitions.","wpu":"FENIX Museum of Migration","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Fenix_loodsen_Katendrecht_Rotterdam_5.jpg/960px-Fenix_loodsen_Katendrecht_Rotterdam_5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Poortgebouw","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.910278,"lo":4.495833,"d":707,"ds":"The Poortgebouw is a national monument located at Stieltjesstraat 38 in the Kop van Zuid area of Rotterdam, the Netherlands. It was built beside the River Maas in 1879 and experienced a range of uses until it was squatted 3 October 1980. It had been standing empty for 2 years and was squatted as a protest intended to highlight the lack of affordable residential housing in Rotterdam.","wpu":"Poortgebouw","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/df/Het_Poortgebouw_%28west%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Cooltoren","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.91455,"lo":4.48086,"d":939,"ds":"The Cooltoren (also known as the CoolTower) is a residential skyscraper in the Cool District of Rotterdam, Netherlands. Built between 2018 and 2022, the tower stands at 153.7 m (504 ft) tall with 51 floors and is the 4th tallest building in the Netherlands.","wpu":"Cooltoren","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/De_Cooltoren_in_de_skyline_van_Rotterdam_-_cropped.jpg/960px-De_Cooltoren_in_de_skyline_van_Rotterdam_-_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Scots International Church","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.9147,"lo":4.4796,"d":995,"ds":"The Scots International Church or the Scottish Church (Dutch: Schotse Kerk) is located in Rotterdam, The Netherlands. An English-language Protestant church in the Presbyterian tradition, it is part of the Church of Scotland, within the Church's International Presbytery.","wpu":"Scots International Church","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Rotterdam_kerk_schotsekerk.jpg/960px-Rotterdam_kerk_schotsekerk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Stieltjeskerk","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.91175,"lo":4.499842,"d":1027,"ds":"The Martyrs of Gorcum Church, also known as the Stieltjeskerk, was a Roman Catholic church in Rotterdam. Rotterdam and its harbor underwent tremendous growth in the second half of the 19th century. The district of Feijenoord was originally built on the south bank of the Meuse (Maas).","wpu":"Stieltjeskerk","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/54/Stieltjeskerk_-_Rotterdam_ca1910.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Kunsthal","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.911,"lo":4.473,"d":1058,"ds":"The Kunsthal (Dutch pronunciation: [ˈkʏnstɦɑl] ; English: Art Hall) is an art space in Rotterdam, the Netherlands. It opened in 1992.","wpu":"Kunsthal","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Kunsthal_Rotterdam.JPG/960px-Kunsthal_Rotterdam.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"De Hef","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.9134,"lo":4.4989,"d":1082,"ds":"De Hef (lit. 'the lift'), officially Koningshaven Bridge, is a vertical-lift bridge over the Koningshaven (Kings Harbor) channel at the port of Rotterdam, Netherlands. Built in 1927, the bridge was part of the Breda–Rotterdam railway line until it was decommissioned in 1993. Today, it is a Rijksmonument heritage site.","wpu":"De Hef","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/De_Hef.jpg/960px-De_Hef.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Museumpark","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.913,"lo":4.474,"d":1113,"ds":"Museumpark is an urban park in Rotterdam, Netherlands, located between the Museum Boijmans Van Beuningen, Westersingel, Westzeedijk and the complex of the Erasmus MC, a medical centre affiliated with the Erasmus University. The park lies on the former land of the Hoboken family, who lived in the building that is since 1987 the Natuurhistorisch Museum Rotterdam.","wpu":"Museumpark","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Museumpark_Rotterdam.jpg/960px-Museumpark_Rotterdam.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Low Light of the Hook of Holland","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.916799,"lo":4.482384,"d":1134,"ds":"The Low Light of the Hook of Holland (Lage licht van Hoek van Holland) is a lighthouse that once stood at the Hook of Holland, but has since been dismantled and reassembled near the Rotterdam maritime museum.","wpu":"Low Light of the Hook of Holland","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f0/Rotterdam_havenmuseum_lage_licht_hvh.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"The Red Apple","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.917222,"lo":4.489167,"d":1146,"ds":"The Red Apple is a 40-storey, 124-metre (407 ft) residential skyscraper on Wijnhaven Island in Rotterdam, Netherlands, designed by KCAP Architects & Planners and Jan des Bouvrie. The building was topped out in 2008, completed in 2009, and features 121 units, and a 338-space multi-storey car park. It is the tenth-tallest building in Rotterdam.","wpu":"The Red Apple","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Red_Apple_Rotterdam.jpg/960px-Red_Apple_Rotterdam.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kunstinstituut Melly","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.9154,"lo":4.4771,"d":1154,"ds":"Kunstinstituut Melly is a contemporary art house located in a former school building on Witte de Withstraat, in Rotterdam, the Netherlands. It was founded in 1990 and originally named after the street it was located on. It presents curated exhibitions, symposiums, live events, educational programs, and has a separate art literature publishing arm.","wpu":"Kunstinstituut Melly","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Witte_de_With_Center_for_Contemporary_Art.TIF/lossy-page1-960px-Witte_de_With_Center_for_Contemporary_Art.TIF.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"The Destroyed City","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.9176,"lo":4.4831,"d":1208,"ds":"The Destroyed City (Dutch: De verwoeste stad) is a bronze memorial sculpture in the Dutch city of Rotterdam. It commemorates the German bombing of Rotterdam on 14 May 1940, which destroyed the medieval centre of the city. Unveiled in 1953, it was designated as a Dutch national monument (Rijksmonument) in 2010.","wpu":"The Destroyed City","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Rotterdam_zadkine_monument.jpg/960px-Rotterdam_zadkine_monument.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Museum Boijmans Van Beuningen","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.914215,"lo":4.473341,"d":1233,"ds":"Municipal Museum Boijmans Van Beuningen (Dutch pronunciation: [myˈzeːjʏm ˈboːimɑns fɑm ˈbøːnɪŋə(n)]) is an art museum in Rotterdam in the Netherlands. The name of the museum is derived from its two most important donors, Frans Jacob Otto Boijmans and Daniël George van Beuningen. The museum is located at the Museumpark in the district Rotterdam Centrum, close to the Kunsthal and the Natural History Museum.","wpu":"Museum Boijmans Van Beuningen","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Museum_Boijmans_Van_Beuningen%2C_Rotterdam%2C_Zuid-Holland.jpg/960px-Museum_Boijmans_Van_Beuningen%2C_Rotterdam%2C_Zuid-Holland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"CasaNova (Rotterdam)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.91817,"lo":4.48724,"d":1242,"ds":"CasaNova is a residential skyscraper in the Maritime District of Rotterdam, Netherlands. Built between 2018 and 2022, the tower stands at 110 m (360 ft) with 35 floors and is the 28th tallest building in the Netherlands.","wpu":"CasaNova (Rotterdam)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/CasaNova_Rotterdam.jpg/960px-CasaNova_Rotterdam.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Baden Powelllaan 12, Rotterdam","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.906806,"lo":4.468472,"d":1271,"ds":"Baden Powelllaan 12 is an old house (Heerenhuis) in Het Park, Rotterdam, built circa 1760. Found near the Euromast it was the main building of the De Heuvel estate. From 1875 this villa forms part of the Park.","wpu":"Baden Powelllaan 12, Rotterdam","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Rotterdam_badenpowelllaan12.jpg/960px-Rotterdam_badenpowelllaan12.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Willemsbrug","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.917222,"lo":4.495556,"d":1279,"ds":"The Willemsbrug (English: \"William Bridge\") is a bridge next to the Erasmusbrug in the centre of Rotterdam, Netherlands, spanning the Nieuwe Maas. It links the northern part of the city with the Noordereiland and (in combination with the Koninginnebrug) the district of Feijenoord. The bridge was completed in 1981, designed by C. Veerling and named after King Willem III of the Netherlands.","wpu":"Willemsbrug","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Rotterdam%2C_Willemsbrug_foto8_2010-11-28_13.40.JPG/960px-Rotterdam%2C_Willemsbrug_foto8_2010-11-28_13.40.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Netherlands Architecture Institute","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.914,"lo":4.471,"d":1345,"ds":"The Netherlands Architecture Institute (NAI) was a cultural institute for architecture and urban development, which comprised a museum, an archive plus library and a platform for lectures and debates. The NAI was established in 1988 and was based in Rotterdam since 1993. It ceased to exist in 2013, when it became part of Het Nieuwe Instituut.","wpu":"Netherlands Architecture Institute","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Nederlands_Architectuurinstituut_%28NAi%29.jpg/960px-Nederlands_Architectuurinstituut_%28NAi%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Witte Huis","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.918833,"lo":4.49175,"d":1355,"ds":"The Witte Huis (Dutch pronunciation: [ˌʋɪtə ˈɦœys]) or White House is a building and National Heritage Site in Rotterdam, Netherlands, built in 1898 in the Art Nouveau style. The building is 43 m (141 ft) tall, with 10 floors. It was the first hoogbouw (literally: high-rise building) in Europe.","wpu":"Witte Huis","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Rotterdam_Het_Witte_Huis_1900.jpg/960px-Rotterdam_Het_Witte_Huis_1900.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Schielandtoren","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.919322,"lo":4.4815,"d":1421,"ds":"The Schielandtoren (Dutch for Schieland Tower) is a 101 metres (331 feet) tall apartment building in the Dutch city Rotterdam. The building is situated along Bulgersteyn, a street, and is part of a city block with shops among other things. The city block is part of the multipurpose complex Beursplein.","wpu":"Schielandtoren","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Rotterdam_toren_schielandtoren.jpg/960px-Rotterdam_toren_schielandtoren.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Euromast","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":51.906111,"lo":4.465833,"d":1455,"ds":"Euromast is an observation tower in Rotterdam, Netherlands, designed by Hugh Maaskant constructed between 1958 and 1960. It was specially built for the 1960 Floriade, and is a listed monument since 2010. The tower is a concrete structure with an internal diameter of 9 m (30 ft) and a wall thickness of 30 cm (12 in).","wpu":"Euromast","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Rotterdam_euromast.jpg/960px-Rotterdam_euromast.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Rotterdam ( ROT-ər-dam, UK also ROT-ər-DAM; Dutch: [ˌrɔtərˈdɑm] ) is the second-largest city in the Netherlands by population and the largest by area (319.4 km2). It is in the province of South Holland, part of the North Sea mouth of the Rhine–Meuse–Scheldt delta, via the New Meuse inland shipping channel, dug to connect to the Meuse at first and now to the Rhine. Rotterdam's history goes back to 1270, when a dam was constructed in the Rotte.","wpu":"Rotterdam","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/A_view_of_Rotterdam%2C_taken_from_the_roof_of_the_Maassilo%2C_Rotterdam%2C_The_Netherlands.jpg/960px-A_view_of_Rotterdam%2C_taken_from_the_roof_of_the_Maassilo%2C_Rotterdam%2C_The_Netherlands.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}